\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{A representation datum for a field}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{A \newvocab{representation datum} specifies a finite discrete
or compact Hausdorff group #{G}, a scalar field #{\mathbb K\in\{\mathbb R,\mathbb C\}},
a finite-dimensional fiber #{F} over #{\mathbb K}, a continuous
representation #{\rho:G\to GL_{\mathbb K}(F)}, and a separately
chosen action #{\alpha:G\to\operatorname{Aut}(X)} on the finite
substrate #{X}. The substrate automorphisms preserve the graph,
weights and any supplied oriented cell data, with orientation changes
interpreted by the established inverse-transport convention. One must
also specify a positive invariant real or Hermitian inner product on
#{F}. The field itself is a map #{\phi:V\to F}.}

\p{An invariant inner product always exists for these groups. From
any positive inner product #{h_0}, define
##{h(v,w)=\int_G h_0(\rho(g)v,\rho(g)w)\,\extd\mu_G(g).}
The [normalized Haar measure](fdip-007B) makes this invariant by a
change of variable. The integral is finite by compactness, and
#{h(v,v)>0} for #{v\ne0}, since #{\rho(g)v\ne0} for every #{g}.
This proves existence of an invariant metric; it does not make that
metric a canonical choice. A selected metric remains part of the
background data.}

\p{The global action associated to the datum is
##{(g\cdot\phi)(v)=\rho(g)\phi(\alpha(g)^{-1}v).}
The two actions have different roles. For a purely internal group,
#{\alpha} is trivial: the group acts on values without moving
vertices. A [local gauge transformation](fdip-006E) instead assigns
an element #{g_v} independently at each vertex and transforms links
by #{U_e\mapsto g_{h(e)}U_eg_{s(e)}^{-1}}. It fixes the substrate;
a global substrate action #{\alpha} cannot simply be applied
independently at each vertex.}

\p{If a field has an additional spin space #{S}, an internal action
on #{S\otimes F} is #{\bbI_S\otimes\rho(g)}. The represented generators
#{\rho(\iota(\varepsilon_j))\otimes\bbI_F} then commute with that action because they
act on different factors. An identification of internal and spin
symmetries, or an action moving the substrate, needs extra compatible
data; it is not implied by the abstract group name or by an inclusion
in #{SU(2)}.}
